  "response": "When a single transition appears compatible with two rival explanations, adjudication begins with the precise content of each theory's claims. Seymour Martin Lipset argued in 1959 that economic development increases the likelihood that a country will be democratic. His \"Some Social Requisites of Democracy\" appeared in the American Political Science Review. The essay carries a publication date of 1972. Adam Przeworski and Fernando Limongi argued that economic development makes existing democracies more likely to survive. A conclusion in their work holds that development reliably causes authoritarian regimes to collapse. By contrast, Carles Boix and Susan Stokes located the effect of wealth in regime change itself, arguing that economic development raises the probability of transitions to democracy; that is, Lipset's original thesis restated as a proposition about the moment of transition rather than about the distribution of regimes.\n\nWhereas the developmental literature makes rising income the engine of democratization, O'Donnell and Schmitter emphasized pacts struck between regime softliners and opposition moderates, treating the outcome as the contingent product of strategic choice under uncertainty. Although a pact-centered story can be told about almost any negotiated transition, and will often fit a case as comfortably as the developmental one, scholars do not resign themselves to indeterminacy: they derive distinct observable implications from rival theories and test them against the single case. Process tracing examines within-case evidence for the causal mechanisms a theory posits.\n\nThe South Korean transition illustrates the procedure. South Korea underwent rapid industrialization in the decades preceding 1987, and the political events that came after that long boom are recorded in unusual detail. Across South Korean cities, mass pro-democracy protests spread in June 1987; as a result, Roh Tae-woo announced on 29 June of that year that the regime accepted direct presidential elections. Those protests resulted in a direct presidential election in December 1987, which took place after the announcement of 29 June.\n\nOn this point the record permits no compromise: either South Korea held a direct presidential election in December 1987, or no direct presidential election was held there in 1987 at all; one of the two must be the case, and the two cannot both hold. Settling such questions of fact and timing is what allows the competing accounts to be weighed, because each predicts a different fine structure of events. The developmental claim expects pressure for liberalization to track the growth of an urban middle class and the organizational capacity it brings; the bargaining account expects documented splits within the ruling coalition and concessions timed to the moderates' leverage. Where both mechanisms leave traces in the same case, the considered verdict is usually that structural change set the terms within which elites bargained, so that the two explanations operate at different levels of the same causal chain rather than one simply displacing the other.",
